steps.body:
With duplicates, an exact match can land on any copy. A **lower bound** finds the first index whose value is at least the target, and an **upper bound** the first index whose value is greater than it. Both use a half-open range: `hi` starts at `a.size()`, one past the end, and the loop runs `while (lo < hi)`:

```cpp
int lowerBound(const std::vector<int>& a, int target) {
    int lo = 0;
    int hi = static_cast<int>(a.size());
    while (lo < hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (a[mid] < target) {
            lo = mid + 1;   // mid is too small: the answer is after it
        } else {
            hi = mid;       // mid might be the answer: keep it
        }
    }
    return lo;
}

std::cout << lowerBound({1, 2, 2, 2, 5}, 2) << ' ' << lowerBound({1, 2, 2, 2, 5}, 9) << '\n';   // 1 5
```

The upper bound is the same function with `a[mid] <= target` in the `if`. Both return `a.size()` when no value qualifies, so check for that before you read `a[lo]`. They compute exactly what `std::lower_bound(v.begin(), v.end(), x) - v.begin()` and `std::upper_bound` give you.

Together they answer the duplicate questions: the target appears `upper - lower` times, its first index is `lower`, and its last index is `upper - 1`. Never write `lo = mid` in this loop. `mid` rounds down, so a range of two indexes would never shrink.

