steps.body:
Binary search also works on answers. Koko eats bananas at a speed of `k` per hour, one pile at a time, so a pile of `p` takes `p / k` hours rounded up. What is the slowest speed that finishes every pile within `h` hours?

Checking one speed is easy, and the checks line up: if speed `k` works, every faster speed works too. So the answers look like `no no no yes yes yes`, and a lower bound over the speeds finds the first `yes`:

```cpp
bool canFinish(const std::vector<int>& piles, long long k, long long h) {
    long long hours = 0;
    for (int p : piles) {
        hours += (p + k - 1) / k;   // p / k rounded up, with no doubles
    }
    return hours <= h;
}

std::vector<int> piles = {3, 6, 7, 11};
long long lo = 1;
long long hi = 11;   // the biggest pile: a faster speed never helps
while (lo < hi) {
    long long mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
    if (canFinish(piles, mid, 8)) {
        hi = mid;
    } else {
        lo = mid + 1;
    }
}
std::cout << lo << '\n';   // 4
```

`(p + k - 1) / k` is ceiling division for positive numbers, and `long long` keeps both the division and the total from overflowing. The bounds are the smallest and the largest answers that could make sense. Each halving runs one check over all n piles, so the search costs O(n log max).

example.code:
#include <algorithm>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

bool canFinish(const std::vector<int>& piles, long long k, long long h) {
    long long hours = 0;
    for (int p : piles) {
        hours += (p + k - 1) / k;
    }
    return hours <= h;
}

int main() {
    int n = 0;
    long long h = 0;
    std::cin >> n >> h;
    std::vector<int> piles(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < n; ++i) {
        std::cin >> piles[i];
    }

    long long lo = 1;
    long long hi = *std::max_element(piles.begin(), piles.end());
    while (lo < hi) {
        long long mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (canFinish(piles, mid, h)) {
            hi = mid;
        } else {
            lo = mid + 1;
        }
    }
    std::cout << "speed " << lo << '\n';
    return 0;
}

