}

steps.body:
A sorted array rotated at an unknown point, like `{4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2}`, is no longer sorted as a whole. But split it at `mid` and at least one half is always sorted, and `a[lo] <= a[mid]` says the left one is. Check whether the target lies inside the sorted half's range. If it does, keep that half, and if not, keep the other:

```cpp
int searchRotated(const std::vector<int>& a, int target) {
    int lo = 0;
    int hi = static_cast<int>(a.size()) - 1;
    while (lo <= hi) {
        int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
        if (a[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        }
        if (a[lo] <= a[mid]) {              // the left half is sorted
            if (a[lo] <= target && target < a[mid]) {
                hi = mid - 1;
            } else {
                lo = mid + 1;
            }
        } else {                            // the right half is sorted
            if (a[mid] < target && target <= a[hi]) {
                lo = mid + 1;
            } else {
                hi = mid - 1;
            }
        }
    }
    return -1;
}

std::cout << searchRotated({4, 5, 6, 7, 0, 1, 2}, 0) << '\n';   // 4
```

It still halves the range on every pass, so it stays O(log n), and an array that was never rotated works too. It needs distinct values: with duplicates, `a[lo] == a[mid]` can't tell which half is sorted.

